<h2>How Texas regulates public adjusters</h2>
<p>Public adjusting is a regulated profession in Texas. Public adjusters are licensed by the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> under <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. A few rules matter most to policyholders:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>License required.</strong> No one may act as, or advertise as, a public adjuster in Texas without a license.</li>
<li><strong>Written contract.</strong> Representation starts only with a written contract on a TDI-approved form. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</li>
<li><strong>72-hour rescission.</strong> You may cancel the contract by written notice within 72 hours of signing it.</li>
<li><strong>"We represent the insured only."</strong> Texas public adjuster contracts must say this in bold type.</li>
<li><strong>You stay on the check.</strong> The insured must be named as a payee on claim checks and must endorse them. A public adjuster may not sign or endorse a claim check for you.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can verify any public adjuster's license through TDI's online license lookup before signing anything. We encourage it.</p>

<h2>Our approach to Coastal Bend claims</h2>
<p>Every property, policy, and loss is handled on its own facts. Our process usually includes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inspection.</strong> A careful look at visible and hidden damage, including the roof, attic, interior, and building systems.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Photos, measurements, and notes, often supported by Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> Reading the declarations, coverages, endorsements, deductibles, and exclusions. On the coast that often means three policies: a homeowners or commercial policy, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a flood policy.</li>
<li><strong>Claim presentation.</strong> A detailed estimate and supporting file submitted to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation and follow-up.</strong> Communicating with the insurer, answering requests, and pursuing <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> when appropriate.</li>
</ol>
<p>You will know who is handling your claim and what happens next.</p>

<h2>Honest communication</h2>
<p>One of the most common frustrations policyholders describe is not knowing where their claim stands. We make communication a priority:</p>
<ul>
<li>Honest assessments of a claim's strengths and weak points</li>
<li>Regular updates as the claim moves forward</li>
<li>Clear explanations of deadlines and next steps</li>
<li>Straight answers, even when they are not what you hoped to hear</li>
</ul>
<p>Sometimes the honest answer is that you do not need a public adjuster. If the insurer's offer looks fair, or the damage is small compared with a percentage wind deductible, we will tell you.</p>

<h2>What we never do, and what we do</h2>
<p>We never:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inflate damage or add items that are not part of the loss</li>
<li>Misrepresent facts to an insurer</li>
<li>Promise a specific settlement amount or outcome</li>
<li>Pressure anyone into signing a contract</li>
</ul>
<p>We do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present legitimate claims thoroughly and accurately</li>
<li>Answer unsupported denials with documentation</li>
<li>Hold the claim to the terms of the policy you paid for</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where our role ends</h2>
<p><strong>We are not attorneys.</strong> We do not give legal advice, file lawsuits, or handle bad-faith litigation. If your situation needs a lawyer, we will say so and suggest you consult a Texas attorney. <strong>We are not contractors.</strong> We do not perform repairs. You choose who repairs your home or building.</p>

<h2>When to call a public adjuster</h2>
<p>Some people call after accepting a settlement based on incomplete information. Others call early and avoid common mistakes. Consider reaching out if:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your claim was <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied</a> or <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid</a></li>
